        "Using a DeFi lending protocol, or almost any DeFi protocol, typically requires you to first grant it a 'token approval' — permission for that protocol's smart contract to move a specified token out of your wallet on your behalf when you interact with it. Many interfaces, by default, request an unlimited approval, letting the contract move any amount of that token, indefinitely, rather than just the specific amount for your current transaction.",
        "An unlimited approval is convenient, because you won't be asked to approve again next time. It's also a standing risk: if that protocol is ever exploited, or if the approval is later misused by a malicious upgrade or a compromised address, an unlimited approval means an attacker isn't limited to whatever you happened to have in your wallet that day — they have permission to take everything of that token you hold, for as long as the approval remains active.",
        "This is Security Rule #6: prefer approving only the specific amount a transaction actually needs, rather than accepting an unlimited default, and periodically review and revoke old approvals you no longer use, using a reputable approval-checking tool. It's a small habit that meaningfully limits your exposure if a protocol you once trusted is later compromised.",
